          \"text\": \"10                                                                                       T. Aguilar-Fraga et al.\\n\\ncars parked to the left of spot z and the remaining n −1 −k cars park to the right of spot z. There are\\n n−1\\n  k  ways to select the cars that park to the left of spot z. Before proceeding with the count, we need to\\nconsider that the preference of car n which parks in spot z is dependent on the number of contiguous cars\\nwhich occupy spots immediately to the left of spot z, as then car n could prefer some of those parking\\nspots. Let L be the number of cars parked immediately to the left of spot z (from the k cars parked\\nsomewhere to the left), with the previous spot empty. Note 0 ≤L ≤k and those cars occupy spots\\nz −L, z −L + 1, . . . , z −1, and spot z −L −1 is empty. From the k cars which parked to the left of\\nspot z, we must select L, which we can do in Lk  ways. The L cars can have |IPFL,L(ℓ)| = |IPFL(ℓ)|\\npossible preferences so as to park in the spots required. Then the remaining k −L cars must park in spots\\n1, 2, . . . , z −L −2, as we must leave spot z −L −1 empty. Those cars can have |IPFk−L,z−L−2(ℓ)|\\npreferences. To ensure car n parks in spot z, we note that the possible preferences depend on both ℓand\\nL. Namely, car n can have min(ℓ, L) + 1 preferences. Hence, the number of ways to park k cars to the\\nleft of spot z so as to satisfy all of these conditions, and so that car n park in spot z, is given by\\n                                            "'!'"\\n       Xk           n −1        k\\n                                                     · (min(ℓ, L) + 1) · |IPFk−L,z−L−2(ℓ)| · |IPFL(ℓ)|   .\\n              k       L\\n                    L=0\\n\\n  Next we consider the cars that park to the right of spot z. Note that those cars are predetermined by the\\nchoice of the cars which park to the left of spot z. Namely, we need not select them, they simply are the\\ncomplement of the choice of the k cars which parked to the left of spot z. Those n −1 −k cars park in\\nspots z + 1, z + 2, . . . , m, hence the number of parking preferences is given by |IPFn−1−k,m−z(ℓ)|.\\n  Now we must sum over 0 ≤k ≤z −1 and over 1 ≤z ≤m which yields the desired formula.   ✷\\n\\n4  Nondecreasing ℓ−interval rational parking functions\\nIn this section, we give recursive formulas to enumerate the set IPF↑m,n(ℓ) consisting of nondecreasing\\nℓ-interval rational parking functions. This enumeration relies on having some empty spots after cars have\\nparked, and thus requires the restriction that there are more spots than cars. The case in which the number\\nof cars equals the number of spots is treated separate and in connection to enumerating Dyck paths.\\n  As before, we simplify notation and let IPF↑n,n(ℓ) = IPF↑n(ℓ) when we have the same number of cars\\nas parking spots.\\n   Utilizing the outcome map for rational parking functions (Deﬁnition 3.1), we can count nondecreasing\\nℓ-interval rational parking functions as follows.\\n\\nTheorem 4.1 Let m, n ∈N+ with m > n and ℓ∈N. Then\\n\\n             X         m−nY\\n                |IPF↑n,m(ℓ)| =                            |IPF↑zi−zi−1−1(ℓ)|\\n                               Z={z1<z2<···<zm−n}⊂[m]   i=1\\n\\nwhere |IPF↑0(ℓ)| = 1.\\n\\nProof: Given that the preference lists are elements in [m]n and are nondecreasing, the outcome map\\nconsists of m −n zeros and the values in [n] appear in sequential order from left to right. Thus each\",
