        \"type\": \"text\",
        \"text\": \"{\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ok\\\",\\n  \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"scholarly://artifact/31caef472e645484fe053760d3e4b74a8b1cdeb1cbdd7b8e1822609077575b9a\\\",\\n  \\\"unit\\\": \\\"pages\\\",\\n  \\\"start\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"total\\\": 29,\\n  \\\"returned\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"next_start\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"items\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"page\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"                 Discrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\\\\n                             . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . vol. 26:1, Permutation Patterns 2023 #10 (2024)\\\\n\\\\n        Interval and ℓ-interval rational parking\\\\n        functions\\\\n2024               Tom´as Aguilar-Fraga1    Jennifer Elder2∗   Rebecca E. Garcia3\\\\n             Kimberly P. Hadaway4   Pamela E. Harris5†  Kimberly J. Harry5Sep              Imhotep B. Hogan6     Jakeyl Johnson7     Jan Kretschmann5\\\\n16      Kobe Lawson-Chavanu8 J. Carlos Mart´ınez Mori9‡Casandra D. Monroe10§\\\\n            Daniel Qui˜nonez11  Dirk Tolson III12 Dwight Anderson Williams II13\\\\n\\\\n                         1 Department of Mathematics, Harvey Mudd College, Claremont, CA, USA\\\\n                         2 Department of Computer Science, Mathematics and Physics, Missouri Western State University, MO, USA\\\\n                         3 Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Colorado College, Colorado Springs, CO, USA\\\\n                         4 Department of Mathematics, Iowa State University, Ames, IA, USA\\\\n                         5 Department of Mathematical Sciences, University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee, Milwaukee, WI, USA\\\\n                         6 Department of Mathematics, Florida A&M University, Tallahassee, FL, USA[math.CO]                  7\\\\n                       Department of Mathematics, San Francisco State University, San Francisco, CA, USA\\\\n                         8 Department of Mathematics, Emory College, Atlanta, GA, USA\\\\n                         9 H. Milton Stewart School of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, USA\\\\n                          10 Department of Mathematics, University of Texas at Austin, Austin, TX, USA\\\\n                          11 Department of Mathematics, UC Irvine, Irvine, CA , USA\\\\n                          12 Department of Mathematics and statistics, Sonoma State University, Rohnert Park, CA, USA\\\\n                          13 Department of Mathematics, Morgan State University, Baltimore, MD, USA\\\\n\\\\n                  revisions 27thNov. 2023, 14thMay 2024, 30thAug. 2024; accepted 30thAug. 2024.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n                     Interval parking functions are a generalization of parking functions in which cars have an interval preference for\\\\n                      their parking. We generalize this deﬁnition to parking functions with n cars and m ≥n parking spots, which\\\\n              we call interval rational parking functions and provide a formula for their enumeration. By specifying an integer\\\\n                  parameter ℓ≥0, we then consider the subset of interval rational parking functions in which each car parks at most\\\\n                 ℓspots away from their initial preference. We call these ℓ-interval rational parking functions and provide recursivearXiv:2311.14055v5\\\\n                  formulas to enumerate this set for all positive integers m ≥n and ℓ. We also establish formulas for the number\\\\n                   of nondecreasing ℓ-interval rational parking functions via the outcome map on rational parking functions. We also\\\\n                   consider the intersection between ℓ-interval parking functions and Fubini rankings and show the enumeration of these\\\\n                     sets is given by generalized Fibonacci numbers. We conclude by specializing ℓ= 1, and establish that the set\\\\n\\\\n                          ∗J. Elder was partially supported through an AWM Mentoring Travel Grant.\\\\n                          †P. E. Harris was supported through a Karen Uhlenbeck EDGE Fellowship.\\\\n                           ‡J. C. Mart´ınez Mori is supported by Schmidt Science Fellows, in partnership with the Rhodes Trust.\\\\n                      §C. D. Monroe was partially supported by NSF GRFP Fellowship.\\\\n\\\\n                ISSN 1365–8050   © 2024 by the author(s)       Distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\\\",\\n      \\\"text_layer_empty\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"page\\\": 2,\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"2                                                                                         T. Aguilar-Fraga et al.\\\\n\\\\nof 1-interval rational parking functions with n cars and m spots are in bijection with the set of barred preferential\\\\narrangements of [n] with m−n bars. This readily implies enumerative formulas. Further, in the case where ℓ= 1, we\\\\nrecover the results of Hadaway and Harris that unit interval parking functions are in bijection with the set of Fubini\\\\nrankings, which are enumerated by the Fubini numbers.\\\\n\\\\nKeywords: Parking function, interval parking function, (n, m)-parking function, Dyck path, (n, m)- Dyck path,\\\\nbarred preferential arrangement, Fubini ranking, Fibonacci number, Fubini number\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n1  Introduction\\\\nThroughout, we let N = {0, 1, 2, 3, . . .}, N+ = {1, 2, 3, . . .}, and for n ∈N+, we let [n] = {1, 2, . . ., n}.\\\\nWe begin by recalling a deﬁnition of parking functions, as introduced by Konheim and Weiss (1966).\\\\nWith n ∈N+, a tuple α = (a1, a2, . . . , an) ∈[n]n is a parking function of length n if its nondecreasing\\\\nrearrangement α′ = (a′1, a′2, . . . , a′n) satisﬁes a′i ≤i for all i ∈[n]. For example, α = (3, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5)\\\\nis a parking function of length six as its nondecreasing rearrangement α′ = (1, 1, 3, 3, 4, 5) satisﬁes the\\\\nrequired inequality conditions. However, α = (2, 3, 3, 3) is not a parking function, as its nondecreasing\\\\nrearrangement α′ = (2, 3, 3, 3) does not satisfy the inequality conditions: In particular, a1 = 2 which\\\\nis not less than or equal to 1. Let PFn ⊆[n]n denote the set of parking functions of length n. Several\\\\nauthors have provided proofs that |PFn| = (n + 1)n−1, see Konheim and Weiss (1966); Pyke (1959);\\\\nStanley (2016a).\\\\n  An alternative deﬁnition of parking functions is as follows. Let the tuple α = (a1, a2, . . . , an) ∈[n]n\\\\nencode the preferred parking spots of n cars, ai ∈[n] being the parking preference of car i, as the\\\\ncars attempt to park one after the other on a one-way street. In this sense, α is called a preference list.\\\\nThroughout the paper, when we refer to the standard parking procedure, we refer to the process that a car\\\\nemploys whenever it enters the street in its attempt to park. Namely, when car i ∈[n] arrives, it attempts to\\\\npark in its preferred spot ai. If spot ai is unoccupied, car i parks there. Otherwise, car i continues driving\\\\ndown the one-way street parking in the ﬁrst unoccupied spot it encounters. If no such spot exists, then\\\\ncar i is unable to park. If all cars are able to park given the preference α, then α is a parking function of\\\\nlength n. Figure 1 illustrates the order in which cars park on the street when α = (3, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5) ∈PF6.\\\\nWhen α ∈PFn, we refer to the resulting parking order of the cars under α as the outcome of α, which\\\\nwe denote by O(α). For α ∈PFn, the outcome O(α) is an element of Sn, the set of permutations of [n].\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n                             2     3     1     4     5     6\\\\n\\\\n                             1     2     3     4     5     6\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n                Fig. 1: The parking outcome of the parking function α = (3, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5) ∈PF6.\\\\n\\\\n  Parking functions have numerous applications and connections to many ﬁelds in mathematics, including\\\\ncomputing volumes of ﬂow polytopes, hyperplane arrangements, and the sorting algorithms Beck et al.\\\\n(2015); Benedetti et al. (2019); Harris et al. (2024). Work on parking functions includes the study of\\\\ndiscrete statistics Adeniran and Pudwell (2023); Gessel and Seo (2004/06); Schumacher (2018), families\\\\nof parking functions with particular desirable properties Aguillon et al. (2023); Elder et al. (2023b), and\\\",\\n      \\\"text_layer_empty\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"page\\\": 3,\\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Interval and ℓ-interval rational parking functions                                           3\\\\n\\\\nmany generalizations of parking functions Colaric et al. (2021); Colmenarejo et al. (2021); Franks et al.\\\\n(2024); Harris et al. (2023). Our work is motivated by two generalizations of parking functions, which we\\\\nrefer to as “rational” parking functions and interval parking functions. We describe those next.\\\\n   First we consider the situation in which there are n ∈N+ cars and m ≥n parking spots on a one-way\\\\nstreet. In this case, a preference α is an element of [m]n, and the set PFn,m consists of the preferences\\\\nallowing all n cars to park under the standard parking procedure. This generalization of parking functions\\\\nis known as (m, n)-parking functions. In the case of n and m being coprime, (m, n)-parking functions are\\\\nreferred to as rational parking functions, see Armstrong et al. (2016). In the current work, we simply refer\\\\nto (m, n)-parking functions as rational parking functions without requiring m and n to be coprime. These\\\\nparking functions were enumerated during the onset of parking function studies (Konheim and Weiss,\\\\n1966, Lemma 2) and, implicitly, in the statistical work of Pyke (1959), giving\\\\n\\\\n                            |PFn,m| = (m + 1 −n) (m + 1)n−1.                               (1)\\\\n\\\\n  Another generalization of parking functions are interval parking functions which describe the case\\\\nwhere cars have an interval of the street in which they want to park. Formally, interval parking functions\\\\nare deﬁned by a pair (α, β), where α = (a1, a2, . . . , an) ∈PFn and β = (b1, b2, . . . , bn) ∈[n]n, satisfy,\\\\nfor all i ∈[n], the inequality ai ≤bi ≤n. For i ∈[n], we call ai the initial preference of car i, bi the\\\\ntolerance of car i (as it is the highest numbered parking spot in which car i would tolerate parking), and\\\\nthe set {ai, ai + 1, . . . , bi} the preference interval for car i. Throughout, we refer to β as the tolerance\\\\nvector. If, for each i ∈[n], car i is able to park in its preference interval following the standard parking\\\\nprocedure, then the pair (α, β) is called an interval parking function. We let IPFn denote the set of\\\\ninterval parking functions of length n. Colaric, DeMuse, Martin, and Yin established (Colaric et al., 2021,\\\\nProposition 3.2):\\\\n                                  |IPFn| = n"'!(n + 1)n−1.                                      (2)'"\\\\n\\\\nNote that for β = (n, n, . . . , n) ∈[n]n, the set of interval parking functions is precisely the set of parking\\\\nfunctions.\\\\n  In the present work, we begin by generalizing interval parking functions to their rational analogue,\\\\nwhich we call interval rational parking functions. Here, there are n cars and m ≥n parking spots on\\\\nthe street.  In this case, we consider the pair of tuples (α, β), where α = (a1, a2, . . . , an) ∈PFn,m\\\\nand β = (b1, b2, . . . , bn) ∈[m]n satisfying ai ≤bi ≤m.  If, for each i ∈[n], car i is able to park in\\\\none of the spots numbered ai, ai + 1, . . . , bi (following the standard parking procedure), then (α, β) is\\\\nan interval rational parking function. We let IPFn,m denote the set of interval rational parking functions\\\\nwith n cars and m spots. For example, ((3, 5, 7, 1, 1), (3, 6, 8, 1, 4)) ∈IPF5,8 is a rational interval parking\\\\nfunction with ﬁve cars and eight parking spots whose parking outcome is illustrated in Figure 2. However,\\\\n((3, 5, 7, 1, 1), (3, 6, 8, 1, 1)) is not an interval rational parking function for any IPF5,m with m ≥8, as\\\\ncar 5 would fail to park in the ﬁrst spot, which is the only spot in its preference interval. In our ﬁrst\\\\nresult, we establish a formula for |IPFn,m| as a sum over rational parking functions involving the parking\\\\noutcome, i.e. the order in which the cars park on the street (Theorem 3.4). Specializing m = n recovers\\\\nthe formula in Equation (2) for the number of interval parking functions.\\\\n  Next, we consider the subset IPFn,m(ℓ) ⊆IPFn,m, which is deﬁned via an integer parameter ℓ≥0,\\\\nand we refer to it as the set of ℓ-interval rational parking functions. In this setting, every car must park\\\\nin its ﬁrst preferred spot or at most ℓspots away from it. That is, if α = (a1, a2, . . . , an) ∈PFn,m,\\\\nand if, for each i ∈[n], car i is able to park in one of the spots ai, ai+1, . . . , min(ai + ℓ, m) (following\\\",\\n      \\\"text_layer_empty\\\": false\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Mechanical text and rendering; image meaning and text accuracy are assessed by the caller\\\"\\n}\"
