          \"text\": \"2                                                                                         T. Aguilar-Fraga et al.\\n\\nof 1-interval rational parking functions with n cars and m spots are in bijection with the set of barred preferential\\narrangements of [n] with m−n bars. This readily implies enumerative formulas. Further, in the case where ℓ= 1, we\\nrecover the results of Hadaway and Harris that unit interval parking functions are in bijection with the set of Fubini\\nrankings, which are enumerated by the Fubini numbers.\\n\\nKeywords: Parking function, interval parking function, (n, m)-parking function, Dyck path, (n, m)- Dyck path,\\nbarred preferential arrangement, Fubini ranking, Fibonacci number, Fubini number\\n\\n\\n1  Introduction\\nThroughout, we let N = {0, 1, 2, 3, . . .}, N+ = {1, 2, 3, . . .}, and for n ∈N+, we let [n] = {1, 2, . . ., n}.\\nWe begin by recalling a deﬁnition of parking functions, as introduced by Konheim and Weiss (1966).\\nWith n ∈N+, a tuple α = (a1, a2, . . . , an) ∈[n]n is a parking function of length n if its nondecreasing\\nrearrangement α′ = (a′1, a′2, . . . , a′n) satisﬁes a′i ≤i for all i ∈[n]. For example, α = (3, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5)\\nis a parking function of length six as its nondecreasing rearrangement α′ = (1, 1, 3, 3, 4, 5) satisﬁes the\\nrequired inequality conditions. However, α = (2, 3, 3, 3) is not a parking function, as its nondecreasing\\nrearrangement α′ = (2, 3, 3, 3) does not satisfy the inequality conditions: In particular, a1 = 2 which\\nis not less than or equal to 1. Let PFn ⊆[n]n denote the set of parking functions of length n. Several\\nauthors have provided proofs that |PFn| = (n + 1)n−1, see Konheim and Weiss (1966); Pyke (1959);\\nStanley (2016a).\\n  An alternative deﬁnition of parking functions is as follows. Let the tuple α = (a1, a2, . . . , an) ∈[n]n\\nencode the preferred parking spots of n cars, ai ∈[n] being the parking preference of car i, as the\\ncars attempt to park one after the other on a one-way street. In this sense, α is called a preference list.\\nThroughout the paper, when we refer to the standard parking procedure, we refer to the process that a car\\nemploys whenever it enters the street in its attempt to park. Namely, when car i ∈[n] arrives, it attempts to\\npark in its preferred spot ai. If spot ai is unoccupied, car i parks there. Otherwise, car i continues driving\\ndown the one-way street parking in the ﬁrst unoccupied spot it encounters. If no such spot exists, then\\ncar i is unable to park. If all cars are able to park given the preference α, then α is a parking function of\\nlength n. Figure 1 illustrates the order in which cars park on the street when α = (3, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5) ∈PF6.\\nWhen α ∈PFn, we refer to the resulting parking order of the cars under α as the outcome of α, which\\nwe denote by O(α). For α ∈PFn, the outcome O(α) is an element of Sn, the set of permutations of [n].\\n\\n\\n                             2     3     1     4     5     6\\n\\n                             1     2     3     4     5     6\\n\\n\\n                Fig. 1: The parking outcome of the parking function α = (3, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5) ∈PF6.\\n\\n  Parking functions have numerous applications and connections to many ﬁelds in mathematics, including\\ncomputing volumes of ﬂow polytopes, hyperplane arrangements, and the sorting algorithms Beck et al.\\n(2015); Benedetti et al. (2019); Harris et al. (2024). Work on parking functions includes the study of\\ndiscrete statistics Adeniran and Pudwell (2023); Gessel and Seo (2004/06); Schumacher (2018), families\\nof parking functions with particular desirable properties Aguillon et al. (2023); Elder et al. (2023b), and\",
