ΨPF→Luk(p)).\\\\n\\\\nExample 2.1. Consider the non-decreasing parking function p = (1, 1, 1, 3, 4, 4, 7, 8, 8, 8, 8, 12) ∈PFinc12 . For each car i, the\\\\ndisplacement di is given by di = oi −pi = i −pi by Fact 1.1, yielding disp (p) = (0, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 0). In particular,\\\\nwe get total displacement |disp (p) | = 13, and maximum displacement max (disp (p)) = 3. The corresponding Łukasiewicz\\\\nword is given by ℓ= ΨPF→Luk(p) = (2, −1, 0, 1, −1, −1, 0, 3, −1, −1, −1, 0), whose lattice path is exactly the Łukasiewicz path\\\\nillustrated in Figure 1.3, which has Area (ℓ) = 13 and Height (ℓ) = 3, as desired.\\\\n\\\\nRemark 2.1. The bijection from non-decreasing parking functions to Dyck paths from Section 1.1 can be similarly deﬁned\\\\nif we consider a Dyck path to be a lattice path from (0, 0) to (2n, 0) with steps U = (1, 1) and D = (1, −1) which never goes\\\\nbelow the x-axis. In this setting, the Dyck path w corresponding to p ∈PFincn  is deﬁned by w = U q1DU q2D · · · U qnD, where\\\",\\n      \\\"text_layer_empty\\\": false,\\n      \\\"image\\\": {\\n        \\\"ref\\\": \\\"scholarly://artifact/158ec08b437bf14aee5c16c827ebd5d20c5aea8742cda3c9ebedaabcdb018456\\\",\\n        \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"c4943ff79304a6ac9f00d1f29c9516fab4064f389acd0b846631d0e82fdb16dc\\\",\\n        \\\"bytes\\\": 294088,\\n        \\\"media_type\\\": \\\"image/png\\\",\\n        \\\"source\\\": \\\"society-journal\\\",\\n        \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://www.dmlett.com/archive/v14/DML24_v14_pp77-84.pdf\\\",\\n        \\\"retrieved_at\\\": \\\"2026-10-21T03:07:41.428427+00:00\\\",\\n        \\\"parent_ref\\\": \\\"scholarly://artifact/a8fc5e85dba8ac66056c18c65d46eb29414ae8daab87f69165a20a464e39f1b7\\\",\\n        \\\"page\\\": 4\\n      }\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Mechanical text and rendering; image meaning and text accuracy are assessed by the caller\\\"\\n}\"
